  "verbalization": "Eplerenone, an antihypertensive agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) through a series of interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Eplerenone may contribute to CAD through its role in increasing serum potassium levels, which can be mediated by Valsartan, an antihypertensive drug that is also a carbonyl compound. This pathway implies that Eplerenone's effects on potassium levels may indirectly influence cardiovascular health, potentially exacerbating CAD in patients with underlying hypertension or other risk factors. Additionally, Eplerenone's ability to elevate cholesterol levels, mediated by Telmisartan, further supports a link between Eplerenone and CAD, as hypercholesterolemia is a well-established risk factor for the disease. The presence of multiple pathways involving Eplerenone and antihypertensive agents underscores the complexity of its role in cardiovascular disease, with the core mechanism likely involving the modulation of potassium and cholesterol homeostasis. These findings suggest that Eplerenone may have a dual impact on CAD through its effects on serum potassium and cholesterol, though further mechanistic validation is required to establish a direct therapeutic relationship.",
